            <ArticleTitle>A case of heterotopic pregnancy in the absence of risk factors. A brief literature review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>285-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To reinforce the risk of heterotopic pregnancy in a natural cycle when intrauterine pregnancy and abdominal pain co-exist.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">A single case report with a literature review.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Our case was associated with significant haemorrhage, laparotomy and salpingectomy. The obstetric outcome was favourable in this case. The literature review demonstrates the diagnostic difficulties as well as treatment options.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Sonographic diagnosis of a heterotopic pregnancy is not always possible. Patients with abdominal pain and intrauterine pregnancy should heighten the clinician's suspicion of a heterotopic pregnancy.</AbstractText>
